WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.840
Good morning, Hank. It's Tuesday.

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:04.400
Today’s video is like the human sternum:
It comes to you in three parts.

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:05.780
Part one: The Manubrium.

00:00:05.790 --> 00:00:11.170
So, the Tenth Annual Project for Awesome (Nerdfighteria’s annual charity event) begins at noon Eastern

00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:12.170
Time on Friday.

00:00:12.170 --> 00:00:16.590
That’s when you should upload your Project
for Awesome videos and submit them to projectforawesome.com,

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:20.700
but the Indiegogo fundraiser will go live
at midnight tonight, and you can get lots

00:00:20.710 --> 00:00:24.990
of great perks for donating, including a Project
for Awesome tote bag, P4A socks, an exclusive

00:00:24.990 --> 00:00:30.360
episode of Dear Hank &amp; John, and an audio
recording of me reading about fifteen pages

00:00:30.369 --> 00:00:31.869
from a thing I’m working on.

00:00:31.869 --> 00:00:32.969
Wait a thing? What thing?

00:00:32.969 --> 00:00:34.749
“Thing” isn’t a very descriptive noun!

00:00:34.749 --> 00:00:36.629
Yeah, I know, it’s intentionally undescriptive.

00:00:36.629 --> 00:00:37.280
Moving on!

00:00:37.290 --> 00:00:38.730
Part Two: The Body.

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:40.280
The middle of the sternum is called the body?

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:42.310
Anatomists are so bad at naming.

00:00:42.310 --> 00:00:45.280
So during the first half of the Project for
Awesome, all the money raised will be split

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:49.620
between Save the Children and Partners in
Health, two organizations doing amazing jobs

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:52.980
of helping the most vulnerable children and
families around the world.

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:56.300
And thanks to matching donations, I am pleased
to announce that during the first half of

00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:59.630
the P4A, your donations will be worth much
more money.

00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:03.230
A group of nerdfighters has gotten together
with Hank and me to offer $300,000 in matching

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:08.040
donations, and generous donors from both Partners
in Health and Save the Children will also

00:01:08.049 --> 00:01:12.280
match your donations, meaning that starting
at midnight tonight, every dollar you donate

00:01:12.280 --> 00:01:15.149
to the Project for Awesome is worth four dollars.

00:01:15.149 --> 00:01:16.569
So thank you to all our matching donors.

00:01:16.569 --> 00:01:20.480
Part Three: The Xiphoid Process, which would
be a pretty good name for a band.

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:21.759
It’s time for my holiday book roundup.

00:01:21.759 --> 00:01:25.320
This year, I want to recommend twelve books
that I think will make excellent holiday gifts.

00:01:25.320 --> 00:01:27.960
Let’s start with my three favorite grownup
fiction books of the year.

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:31.380
First, Underground Railroad by Colson Whitehead,
which just won the National Book Award.

00:01:31.389 --> 00:01:35.579
It is a haunting and brilliant novel about
American slavery that imagines the underground

00:01:35.579 --> 00:01:38.310
railroad as an actual underground railroad.

00:01:38.310 --> 00:01:41.280
Second, Moonglow by Michael Chabon (Chabon?).

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:44.659
One of the problems with being a proper genius
like Michael Chabon is that after you publish

00:01:44.659 --> 00:01:48.630
several nearly perfect novels, people start
saying, “Oh yeah, it’s another nearly perfect movel;

00:01:48.630 --> 00:01:49.849
he’s written a bunch of those.”

00:01:49.849 --> 00:01:54.309
But no, this is an amazing book about war
and memory and mental illness. I loved it.

00:01:54.309 --> 00:01:58.800
And lastly, What Is Not Yours Is Not Yours
by Helen Oyeyemi, which is just a transfixing

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:00.810
book of fairy tale-like short stories.

00:02:00.810 --> 00:02:03.990
I promise you have never read anything like
it before.

00:02:03.990 --> 00:02:08.619
For Sci-Fi fans, another story collection
recommendation: Ted Chiang’s Stories of Your Life and Others.

00:02:08.619 --> 00:02:11.999
One of the stories in that book was the basis
for the wonderful movie Arrival, but they’re

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.900
all mind-blowingly good.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:17.239
My other favorite Sci-Fi of the year was Nnedi
Okorafor’s Binti, which is about a girl

00:02:17.239 --> 00:02:20.000
who has to travel across the galaxy to attend
university.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:22.959
It’s page-turny and exciting, but ultimately
really moving.

00:02:22.959 --> 00:02:23.719
Young adult fiction!

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:27.360
I liked a lot of young adult books this year,
but I’m gonna limit myself to three recommendations.

00:02:27.370 --> 00:02:31.510
First, Ember in the Ashes, a thrilling and
politically astute dystopian series.

00:02:31.510 --> 00:02:34.819
Then there’s The Raven Cycle by Maggie Stiefvater
for fantasy fans.

00:02:34.819 --> 00:02:35.819
It’s just amazing.

00:02:35.819 --> 00:02:39.769
And if you’re looking for a good YA love
story, I recommend The Sun Is Also a Star.

00:02:39.769 --> 00:02:42.860
For fans of YouTubers, there are lots of great
books out there by YouTubers, but I wanna

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:47.590
highlight Hannah Hart’s Buffering because
I think it’s a beautifully written and extraordinarily

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:48.700
touching memoir.

00:02:48.700 --> 00:02:49.520
Okay, nonfiction.

00:02:49.520 --> 00:02:50.840
Two stand out for me this year.

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:55.160
First, I Contain Multitudes, a history of
microbes and humanity’s relationship with them.

00:02:55.160 --> 00:02:58.980
That is a fascinating book, even if, like
myself, you are not thrilled by the notion

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:02.800
that half the cells inside of your body are
not, strictly speaking, yours.

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:07.420
And secondly, Sapiens, a sweeping history
of the last 250 thousand or so years that

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:11.769
explores how humans became the dominant species
on the planet, and also what our lives looked

00:03:11.769 --> 00:03:15.949
like back when we were, in the author’s
phrase, “an animal of no significance.”

00:03:15.949 --> 00:03:19.880
And lastly, you know how you have, or I hope
you have, a quirky and delightful friend who

00:03:19.890 --> 00:03:22.340
is just the most fascinating person you know?

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:25.060
I have the perfect gift book for that person.

00:03:25.060 --> 00:03:30.720
Textbook Amy Krouse Rosenthal, a wonderfully
eccentric memoir that is enhanced by texting, really.

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:32.680
So Hank, those are twelve books I recommend
this holiday season.

00:03:32.689 --> 00:03:36.320
You will see me on Friday for the Tenth Annual
Project for Awesome!

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:37.440
Endscreen!

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:40.239
Here’s a couple guides on how to make a
good Project for Awesome Video, and a link

00:03:40.239 --> 00:03:42.220
to the Indiegogo will be here once it’s
live.

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:45.030
And of course Self-Care Bunny hope you’re
being nice to yourself and eating your carrots,

00:03:45.030 --> 00:03:46.650
unless you’re allergic to carrots.

